<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-discrete-probability" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Discrete Probability</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>3.0 Statistics and Probability</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>3.2 Probability II</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title>
                    <p>Determine discrete and continuous probabilities in different situations</p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>11</p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>40 minutes</p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>12 minutes</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    Learners build a probability distribution table from experimental frequency data, discovering the two defining properties — each value in [0,1] and the sum equal to 1.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Do not define <q>random variable</q> yet. Let learners treat the table as simply <q>a way to record probabilities</q>. After part (c), ask: <q>Do these relative frequencies behave like probabilities? How do you know?</q>
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <activity xml:id="act-matatus-at-a-stop">
        <title>Matatus at a Stop</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Koech stands at a matatu stop and counts how many matatus pass in a 5-minute window, once each day for 40 days. His results are shown below.
            </p>
            <table xml:id="table-matatu-frequencies">
                <title>Matatu counts over 40 days</title>
                <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
                        <cell>Number of matatus (<m>x</m>)</cell>
                        <cell>Frequency</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row><cell>0</cell><cell>2</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>1</cell><cell>8</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>2</cell><cell>16</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>3</cell><cell>10</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>4</cell><cell>4</cell></row>
                    <row bottom="medium"><cell>Total</cell><cell>40</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Copy the table and add a third column headed <q>Relative frequency</q>. For each row, compute the relative frequency by dividing the frequency by 40.
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                The completed column gives: 0.05, 0.20, 0.40, 0.25, 0.10.
                                Encourage groups to simplify fractions if they prefer: e.g. <m>\frac{2}{40} = 0.05</m>.
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            If a 5-minute window is selected at random from those 40 days, estimate the probability that exactly 2 matatus pass.
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                <m>P(\text{exactly 2}) \approx 0.40</m> (using the relative frequency as an estimate).
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Add up all the relative frequencies in your column. What is the total? Is each individual relative frequency between 0 and 1?
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                The total is <m>0.05 + 0.20 + 0.40 + 0.25 + 0.10 = 1.00</m>. Every value is between 0 and 1.
                                These two facts are the defining properties of a probability distribution — make sure learners record them.
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Let <m>X</m> = the number of matatus that pass in a randomly chosen 5-minute window. What values can <m>X</m> take? Can <m>X</m> ever equal 2.5? Why or why not?
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                <m>X</m> can take the values 0, 1, 2, 3, 4. <m>X</m> cannot equal 2.5 because we count matatus in whole numbers — you cannot have half a matatu. This makes <m>X</m> a <term>discrete</term> random variable.
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </activity>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>12 minutes</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To formalise the vocabulary: random variable, discrete, probability distribution, and the two PDF validity conditions.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Return to the activity table. Name it: the table is a <term>probability distribution</term> for the variable <m>X</m>. Introduce uppercase/lowercase convention: <m>X</m> is the random variable (the experiment), <m>x</m> is a specific value it takes (a result).
                </p>
                <p>
                    Write the two PDF conditions on the board and point to the activity as evidence of both. Then contrast discrete with continuous: <q>We counted matatus — whole numbers. Height, time, and temperature are measured — they can take any value in a range. Those are continuous, and we study them in the next two sections.</q>
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <insight>
        <title>Key Ideas</title>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        A <term>random variable</term> <m>X</m> assigns a numerical value to each outcome of a random experiment. Uppercase <m>X</m> names the variable; lowercase <m>x</m> denotes a specific value it takes.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        A <term>discrete random variable</term> takes values that can be listed and counted: 0, 1, 2, 3, … These values are obtained by counting, not measuring.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        A <term>probability distribution</term> (or probability distribution function, PDF) for a discrete random variable is a table that gives <m>P(X = x)</m> for each possible value of <m>x</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        A table is a valid PDF if and only if both of the following hold:
                        <ol>
                            <li><p>Each probability satisfies <m>0 \leq P(X = x) \leq 1</m>.</p></li>
                            <li><p>The probabilities sum to 1: <m>\displaystyle\sum_{\text{all } x} P(X = x) = 1</m>.</p></li>
                        </ol>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        To find the probability that <m>X</m> falls in a range, <em>sum the relevant rows</em> of the table. For example:
                        <md>P(X \geq 2) = P(X=2) + P(X=3) + \cdots</md>
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </insight>

    <p>
        In the next two sections we study <term>continuous random variables</term> — quantities such as temperature, height, or waiting time that are measured rather than counted, and can take any value in a range. The ideas from this section carry over, but instead of a table we use a curve to represent the distribution.
    </p>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example>
        <title>Verify a PDF and Read Probabilities</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Wanjiru runs a kiosk. She records the number of items <m>X</m> that a customer buys per visit. Based on her records, she estimates the following probabilities:
            </p>
            <table>
                <title>Items purchased per customer visit</title>
                <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
                        <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                        <cell>0</cell>
                        <cell>1</cell>
                        <cell>2</cell>
                        <cell>3</cell>
                        <cell>4</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><m>P(X=x)</m></cell>
                        <cell>0.05</cell>
                        <cell>0.20</cell>
                        <cell>0.40</cell>
                        <cell>0.25</cell>
                        <cell>0.10</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>Verify that this is a valid probability distribution.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Find <m>P(X \geq 3)</m>.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Each probability lies in <m>[0, 1]</m>. <m>\checkmark</m>
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            The sum of all probabilities is
                            <md>0.05 + 0.20 + 0.40 + 0.25 + 0.10 = 1.00. \quad \checkmark</md>
                            Both conditions hold, so this is a valid PDF.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <md>P(X \geq 3) = P(X = 3) + P(X = 4) = 0.25 + 0.10 = 0.35.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>Find a Missing Probability</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                The probability distribution for the number of goals <m>X</m> a football team scores per match is partially given below. One entry is missing.
            </p>
            <table>
                <title>Goals per match</title>
                <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
                        <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                        <cell>0</cell>
                        <cell>1</cell>
                        <cell>2</cell>
                        <cell>3</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><m>P(X=x)</m></cell>
                        <cell>0.20</cell>
                        <cell>0.45</cell>
                        <cell>?</cell>
                        <cell>0.10</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>Find the missing probability.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Hence find <m>P(X \geq 1)</m>.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Since all probabilities must sum to 1:
                            <md>0.20 + 0.45 + p + 0.10 = 1 \implies p = 1 - 0.75 = 0.25.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Using the complement rule:
                            <md>P(X \geq 1) = 1 - P(X = 0) = 1 - 0.20 = 0.80.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>Reading Probabilities from a Full Table</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Akello records the number of days per week she exercises. Her probability distribution is shown below.
            </p>
            <table>
                <title>Days per week Akello exercises</title>
                <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
                        <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                        <cell>0</cell>
                        <cell>1</cell>
                        <cell>2</cell>
                        <cell>3</cell>
                        <cell>4</cell>
                        <cell>5</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><m>P(X=x)</m></cell>
                        <cell>0.05</cell>
                        <cell>0.10</cell>
                        <cell>0.25</cell>
                        <cell>0.35</cell>
                        <cell>0.20</cell>
                        <cell>0.05</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
            <p>
                Find: (a) <m>P(X = 3)</m>, (b) <m>P(X &lt; 2)</m>, (c) <m>P(2 \leq X \leq 4)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>P(X = 3) = 0.35</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>P(X &lt; 2) = P(X = 0) + P(X = 1) = 0.05 + 0.10 = 0.15</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>P(2 \leq X \leq 4) = P(X=2) + P(X=3) + P(X=4) = 0.25 + 0.35 + 0.20 = 0.80</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>Finding an Unknown Constant</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A discrete random variable <m>X</m> takes the values 1, 2, 3, 4, and its probability distribution is given by <m>P(X = x) = kx</m> for a constant <m>k</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>Find the value of <m>k</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Write out the full probability distribution as a table.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Find <m>P(X \geq 3)</m>.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Since all probabilities must sum to 1:
                            <md>k(1) + k(2) + k(3) + k(4) = 10k = 1 \implies k = 0.1.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Substituting <m>k = 0.1</m>:
                        </p>
                        <table>
                            <title>Probability distribution for <m>X</m></title>
                            <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                                <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
                                    <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                                    <cell>1</cell>
                                    <cell>2</cell>
                                    <cell>3</cell>
                                    <cell>4</cell>
                                </row>
                                <row>
                                    <cell><m>P(X=x)</m></cell>
                                    <cell>0.1</cell>
                                    <cell>0.2</cell>
                                    <cell>0.3</cell>
                                    <cell>0.4</cell>
                                </row>
                            </tabular>
                        </table>
                        <p>
                            Check: <m>0.1 + 0.2 + 0.3 + 0.4 = 1.0</m>. <m>\checkmark</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <md>P(X \geq 3) = P(X = 3) + P(X = 4) = 0.3 + 0.4 = 0.7.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />

    <exercises>
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A spinner has three sections. Let <m>X</m> = the section the spinner lands on. The probabilities are:
                </p>
                <table>
                    <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
                            <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                            <cell>1</cell>
                            <cell>2</cell>
                            <cell>3</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell><m>P(X=x)</m></cell>
                            <cell>0.5</cell>
                            <cell>0.3</cell>
                            <cell>0.2</cell>
                        </row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>Verify that this is a valid probability distribution.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Find <m>P(X \geq 2)</m>.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Each value is in <m>[0,1]</m>, and <m>0.5 + 0.3 + 0.2 = 1.0</m>. <m>\checkmark</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>P(X \geq 2) = 0.3 + 0.2 = 0.5</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A baker records how many of his four bread types sell out each day. Let <m>X</m> = the number of types that sell out. Part of the distribution is shown below.
                </p>
                <table>
                    <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
                            <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                            <cell>0</cell>
                            <cell>1</cell>
                            <cell>2</cell>
                            <cell>3</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell><m>P(X=x)</m></cell>
                            <cell>0.10</cell>
                            <cell>0.35</cell>
                            <cell>0.30</cell>
                            <cell>?</cell>
                        </row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
                <p>
                    Find the missing probability. Is this a valid PDF?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>P(X = 3) = 1 - 0.10 - 0.35 - 0.30 = 0.25</m>. Yes — all values in <m>[0,1]</m> and sum is 1.
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Otieno records the number of goals <m>X</m> his favourite team scores per match over a season.
                </p>
                <table>
                    <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
                            <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                            <cell>0</cell>
                            <cell>1</cell>
                            <cell>2</cell>
                            <cell>3</cell>
                            <cell>4</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell><m>P(X=x)</m></cell>
                            <cell>0.10</cell>
                            <cell>0.30</cell>
                            <cell>0.35</cell>
                            <cell>0.20</cell>
                            <cell>0.05</cell>
                        </row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>Verify that this is a valid PDF.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Find <m>P(X \geq 2)</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Find <m>P(1 \leq X \leq 3)</m>.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Each value is in <m>[0,1]</m>, and <m>0.10+0.30+0.35+0.20+0.05 = 1.00</m>. <m>\checkmark</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>P(X \geq 2) = 0.35 + 0.20 + 0.05 = 0.60</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>P(1 \leq X \leq 3) = 0.30 + 0.35 + 0.20 = 0.85</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A discrete random variable <m>X</m> takes the values 0, 1, 2, 3, with <m>P(X = x) = k(x+1)</m> for a constant <m>k</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>Find <m>k</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Find <m>P(X \geq 2)</m>.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>k(1+2+3+4) = 10k = 1 \implies k = 0.1</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>P(X \geq 2) = P(X=2) + P(X=3) = 3(0.1) + 4(0.1) = 0.3 + 0.4 = 0.7</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Three fair coins are tossed simultaneously. Let <m>X</m> = the number of heads obtained.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Construct the probability distribution table for <m>X</m>. (<em>Hint:</em> use combinations to count the number of ways to get each value of <m>x</m> out of the <m>2^3 = 8</m> equally-likely outcomes.)
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li><p>Verify that your table is a valid PDF.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Find <m>P(X \geq 2)</m>.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The number of ways to get <m>x</m> heads from 3 coins is <m>{}^{3}C_{x}</m>:
                            </p>
                            <table>
                                <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
                                        <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                                        <cell>0</cell>
                                        <cell>1</cell>
                                        <cell>2</cell>
                                        <cell>3</cell>
                                    </row>
                                    <row>
                                        <cell><m>P(X=x)</m></cell>
                                        <cell><m>\frac{1}{8}</m></cell>
                                        <cell><m>\frac{3}{8}</m></cell>
                                        <cell><m>\frac{3}{8}</m></cell>
                                        <cell><m>\frac{1}{8}</m></cell>
                                    </row>
                                </tabular>
                            </table>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Each value is in <m>[0,1]</m>, and <m>\frac{1}{8}+\frac{3}{8}+\frac{3}{8}+\frac{1}{8} = \frac{8}{8} = 1</m>. <m>\checkmark</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>P(X \geq 2) = \frac{3}{8} + \frac{1}{8} = \frac{4}{8} = \frac{1}{2}</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

